<h1>Video 009 — Arrays, part 4</h1>
<h2>Video info</h2>
<p>Title: Core Java with OCJP/SCJP: Language Fundamentals Part-9 || Arrays Part-4</p>
<p>YouTube has captions disabled on this upload. Video 008 ends by summarizing array element assignments and writing the heading array variable assignments. This lecture is that next heading: when one array reference is assigned to another.</p>
<h2>What this lecture covers</h2>
<ol><li>Element assignment vs variable assignment</li></ol>
<ol><li>Assigning one array reference to another (same type, same dimension)</li></ol>
<ol><li>What happens to the old object (GC)</li></ol>
<ol><li>Dimension mismatch (int[] vs int[][])</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child-type arrays assigned to parent-type arrays (String[] → Object[])</li></ol>
<ol><li>Why char[] cannot be assigned to int[] (promotion is for elements, not for the array type)</li></ol>
<ol><li>3-D / jagged leftovers and exam patterns</li></ol>
<ol><li>Close of the arrays topic</li></ol>
<h2>Detailed notes</h2>
<h3>Two different “assignments”</h3>
<p>Durga keeps these on two boards:</p>
<p>Element rules (recap from 008, still in force):</p>
<ul><li>Primitive array: any type that can be implicitly promoted to the declared element type (byte into int[], etc.).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Object array: declared type or child objects.</li></ul>
<ul><li>Abstract-class array: child objects (Number[] can hold Integer, Double, …).</li></ul>
<ul><li>Interface array: implementation objects (Runnable[] can hold Thread).</li></ul>
<p>This lecture is not “what goes in the slot.” It is “when can I write leftArray = rightArray?”</p>
<h3>Same type, same dimension — always allowed</h3>
<p>int[] a = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
int[] b = {60, 70};
a = b;</p>
<p>Both are int[] (one-dimensional). Assignment is valid.</p>
<p>After `a = b`:</p>
<ul><li>a and b point to the same array object {60, 70}.</li></ul>
<ul><li>a.length is now 2, not 5.</li></ul>
<ul><li>The old {10, 20, 30, 40, 50} object has no reference → eligible for GC.</li></ul>
<p>System.out.println(a[0]); // 60
b[0] = 99;
System.out.println(a[0]); // 99 — same object</p>
<p>Sizes do not have to match. Only the type and dimension have to match. {10,20,30,40,50} and {60,70} have different lengths; assignment is still legal because both are int[].</p>
<h3>Dimension must match</h3>
<p>int[] a = new int[3];
int[][] b = new int[2][3];

a = b;      // CE: incompatible types — int[] vs int[][]
b = a;      // CE
a = b[0];   // valid — b[0] is int[]
b[0] = a;   // valid</p>
<p>How to see it: b is an array of int[]. So b[0] has type int[], which matches a.</p>
<p>Three-dimensional:</p>
<p>int[][][] x = new int[2][3][4];
int[][] y;
int[] z;

y = x[0];   // valid — x[0] is int[][]
z = x[0][0]; // valid — x[0][0] is int[]
z = x[0];   // CE — int[] vs int[][]</p>
<p>Exam pattern: “which assignments compile?” with a mix of a, a[0], a[0][0].</p>
<h3>Element-level promotion does **not** apply to the array variable</h3>
<p>This is the trap Durga builds toward.</p>
<p>At element level, char promotes to int:</p>
<p>int[] a = new int[3];
a[0] = &#x27;a&#x27;;   // valid — char → int</p>
<p>At variable level, char[] is not a subtype of int[]:</p>
<p>char[] ch = {&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;, &#x27;c&#x27;};
int[] i = {10, 20, 30};
i = ch;   // CE: incompatible types
ch = i;   // CE</p>
<p>Why: an array’s type is char[] or int[]. Those are different objects in the type system. Promotion rules are for values, not for array types.</p>
<p>Same idea:</p>
<p>byte[] b = {10, 20};
int[] i = {30, 40};
i = b;    // CE</p>
<p>Even though byte promotes to int.</p>
<h3>Object arrays and children — variable assignment **is** allowed</h3>
<p>Arrays are objects. For reference element types, Java allows:</p>
<p>child-type array → parent-type array variable</p>
<p>String[] s = {&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;, &quot;C&quot;};
Object[] o = s;     // valid</p>
<p>String is a child of Object, so String[] may be assigned to Object[].</p>
<p>The reverse needs a cast and can blow up at runtime:</p>
<p>Object[] o = new Object[]{&quot;A&quot;, &quot;B&quot;};
String[] s = (String[]) o;  // compiles; OK here because elements are Strings

Object[] o2 = new Object[]{new Object(), &quot;A&quot;};
String[] s2 = (String[]) o2; // compiles; ClassCastException at runtime</p>
<p>Without a cast:</p>
<p>String[] s = o;  // CE</p>
<p>More Durga examples:</p>
<p>Runnable[] r = new Runnable[5];
r[0] = new Thread();
Object[] o = r;     // valid — Runnable[] to Object[]

Number[] n = new Number[3];
n[0] = new Integer(10);
n[1] = new Double(10.5);
Object[] o2 = n;    // valid</p>
<p>Thread[] t = new Thread[5];
Runnable[] r = t;   // valid — Thread implements Runnable
t = r;              // CE without cast</p>
<h3>After parent = child, be careful writing elements</h3>
<p>String[] s = new String[3];
Object[] o = s;
o[0] = new Integer(10);  // compiles (Object slot) — ArrayStoreException at runtime</p>
<p>Because the real object is still a String[]. The compiler only sees Object[]. JVM checks the actual component type on store.</p>
<p>This is the array version of polymorphism + heap check. Durga uses it as an exam bomb.</p>
<h3>Jagged leftovers</h3>
<p>Still legal after assignment:</p>
<p>int[][] a = new int[3][];
a[0] = new int[2];
a[1] = new int[4];
a[0] = new int[]{10, 20, 30}; // replace entire row (anonymous array from part 3)</p>
<p>a[0] is an int[] variable. Assigning another int[] to it is variable assignment at the row level.</p>
<h3>`null` assignment</h3>
<p>int[] a = {10, 20, 30};
a = null;   // valid — a is a reference
// a[0] = 10;  // NPE at runtime</p>
<p>Primitive arrays are still objects. The name a can hold null. You cannot write int x = null.</p>
<h3>What becomes eligible for GC</h3>
<pre class='code'>int[] a = new int[3];
int[] b = new int[4];
a = b;</pre>
<p>The new int[3] object is eligible for GC (unless some other name still points to it).</p>
<pre class='code'>int[][] x = new int[2][3];
x[0] = null;</pre>
<p>Only that inner int[3] row is dropped, not the whole 2-D object.</p>
<h3>Summary table Durga wants on one slide</h3>
<h2>Code from the lecture</h2>
<pre class='code'>class ArrayVarAssign {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        int[] a = {10, 20, 30, 40, 50};
        int[] b = {60, 70};
        a = b;
        System.out.println(a.length); // 2

        int[][] m = new int[2][3];
        a = m[0];                     // valid
        // a = m;                     // CE

        char[] ch = {&#x27;a&#x27;, &#x27;b&#x27;};
        // a = ch;                    // CE

        String[] s = {&quot;durga&quot;, &quot;soft&quot;};
        Object[] o = s;               // valid
        System.out.println(o[0]);     // durga
        // o[0] = 10;                 // compiles, ArrayStoreException
    }
}</pre>
<h2>Rules to memorize</h2>
<ol><li>Array variable assignment requires matching type + dimension, not matching length.</li></ol>
<ol><li>a = b makes both names point to the same object; old object may be GC’d.</li></ol>
<ol><li>int[] and int[][] never assign to each other; use b[0] to drop one dimension.</li></ol>
<ol><li>Primitive promotion (char → int) does not make char[] assignable to int[].</li></ol>
<ol><li>Child[] is assignable to Parent[] (String[] → Object[], Thread[] → Runnable[]).</li></ol>
<ol><li>Storing the wrong runtime element into that parent view → ArrayStoreException.</li></ol>
<h2>Exam / interview traps</h2>
<ul><li>Lengths 5 and 2: assignment still compiles.</li></ul>
<ul><li>char[] to int[]: students say valid because char promotes. It is CE.</li></ul>
<ul><li>String[] to Object[]: valid.</li></ul>
<ul><li>o[0] = new Integer(10) after Object[] o = stringArray: compile OK, ArrayStoreException.</li></ul>
<ul><li>a = b[0] vs a = b for 2-D b.</li></ul>
<h2>Recap</h2>
<p>Arrays topic in this series: declaration → creation → initialization → length vs length() → anonymous arrays → element assignments → variable assignments. Next: types of variables.</p>
<p>Next video: 010-types-of-variables-part-1.md</p>
